The initial r51x8 arithmetic is a System V AMD64 / GNU assembly translation
of the independently implemented Narya r51x5 Go-ABI assembly at commit:

  https://github.com/Overclock-Validator/narya-ed25519/commit/eff4c8ddafdfe0448eb50f2eafc723a42b95fe2c

The translation changes calling convention, assembler syntax, public data
layout documentation, and test harnesses while intentionally retaining the
same field representation and native instruction schedule.

The x8 rolling-register SHA-512 compression leaf is a System V AMD64 / GNU
assembly translation of Narya's multi-buffer SHA-512 kernel at commit:

  https://github.com/Overclock-Validator/narya-ed25519/commit/13dc06d4a5f0ad8be62412a89de3a44de57bd504

Its calling convention and assembler syntax were translated; its eighty-round
schedule and lane orientation were retained and checked against a separate
scalar implementation plus the FIPS SHA-512 empty-message digest.

The signed radix-2^21 scalar-reduction leaf is a System V AMD64 / GNU
assembly translation of Narya's fixed-array reducer at the same commit:

  https://github.com/Overclock-Validator/narya-ed25519/commit/13dc06d4a5f0ad8be62412a89de3a44de57bd504

That implementation uses the public-domain ref10 reduction identity. The
standalone test oracle is separately structured as bit-at-a-time modular
reduction and does not reuse the ref10 limbs, constants, carries, or packing.

The micro-AoS projective-Niels transpose is a System V AMD64 / GNU assembly
translation of Narya's Go-ABI selector at:

  https://github.com/Overclock-Validator/narya-ed25519/commit/13dc06d4a5f0ad8be62412a89de3a44de57bd504

The standalone translation changes its internal ABI to accept an eight-entry
pointer vector, documents the exact permutation, and tests every source cell
and active mask independently.

This checkpoint does not include Narya's separate internal/r43x6 backend or
copy Firedancer source. Later ports must update this notice before adding code
with additional provenance.

OpenAI Codex and ChatGPT Pro, together with Anthropic Claude, assisted with
implementation, profiling analysis, mathematical proof planning, tests,
documentation, and review. Project-owned tests, machine-checked statements,
reproducible measurements, and independent human review—not model output—are
the evidence for claims made by this repository.

The scalar-reduction canonical-tail derivation, positional-certificate gap,
and adversarial bound witnesses were identified in a proof-oriented ChatGPT
Pro review. Codex integrated them as a pinned source transcript, negative
mutation gates, deterministic vector generator, and Lean theorems; those
repository checks—not the model transcript—are the maintained evidence.


